<DetailLayout image="/images/yulong.webp" title="Yulong River Bamboo Rafting" subtitle="A quieter, more intimate bamboo raft than the Li River — past ancient stone bridges, water buffalo, and karst peaks." description="Complete guide to Yulong River bamboo rafting: the best sections, 2026 prices, how to get there, Yulong vs Li River comparison, and tips for your trip.">
  <h2>Yulong River vs Li River</h2>
  <p>The Li River is the famous one — big boats, lots of people, the classic postcard view. The Yulong River (also called the Dragon River) is its smaller, quieter tributary. Instead of motorized boats, the Yulong uses only traditional bamboo rafts poled by a boatman standing at the back. The experience is more intimate — just you and one other passenger on a narrow raft, gliding past ancient stone bridges, water buffalo cooling themselves in the shallows, and karst peaks that feel close enough to touch. No engine noise, no crowds, no fixed schedule.</p>
  <p>If you have time for both rivers, do the Yulong first as a warm-up and the Li River as the grand finale. Doing them in reverse order can make the Yulong feel anticlimactic. If you only have time for one: pick the Li River for the iconic scenery, or the Yulong for the quieter, more personal experience.</p>

  <h2>Best Sections</h2>
  <p>The most popular route runs from Yulong Bridge to Gongnong Bridge, taking about 1.5 hours. You drift under several ancient stone bridges — some dating back to the Ming Dynasty (14th-17th century) — past water wheels, and through gentle "rapids" that are really small drops designed to get your feet wet and make you laugh. Each raft holds two passengers plus the boatman.</p>
  <p>An alternative section runs from Chaoyang Pier to Gongnong Bridge, slightly shorter at about 1 hour. Both routes end at the same place near the Ten Mile Gallery road, making it easy to continue by bike afterward.</p>

  <h2>Practical Info</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cost:</strong> 150-200 RMB per raft (not per person), so 75-100 RMB per person with a partner</li>
    <li><strong>Piers:</strong> Yulong Bridge and Chaoyang Pier are the two main starting points. Both are reachable by bike from Yangshuo (20-30 min) or taxi (40 RMB).</li>
    <li><strong>Best time:</strong> 8-10 AM. After 10 AM queues can build in peak season. Morning mist on the river adds atmosphere and softens the light for photos.</li>
    <li><strong>What to wear:</strong> Sandals or shoes you don't mind getting wet. The rafts splash a little water at the small drops. Shorts are better than long pants. Bring a dry bag for your phone and camera.</li>
    <li><strong>Season:</strong> Operates year-round but best in spring and autumn. Heavy summer rain can raise water levels and temporarily close the rafts — check conditions the day before. Winter is cold on the water — bring layers.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 p-6">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-400">Photo Tip</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-stone-700">The best photo from the Yulong River isn't from the raft — it's from Gongnong Bridge looking down at the rafts passing underneath with the karst backdrop. Time your ride so you're approaching the bridge around 8-9 AM when the morning light is soft and the rafts are spaced far apart.</p>
